<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro" name="realsense_d456">

    <!--
      Intel RealSense D456 accessory for the CIRCE rover.

      The D456 is the IP65 / M12-connector variant of the D455, with identical
      optics, baseline and IMU, so realsense2_description's sensor_d455 macro is
      the correct model. There is deliberately no _d456.urdf.xacro upstream.

      use_nominal_extrinsics is FALSE on hardware and TRUE in sim - it is wired to
      $(arg sim_mode) below. False means this macro emits only
      camera_bottom_screw_frame and camera_link; realsense2_camera then publishes
      camera_link -> camera_{depth,color,accel,gyro}_* itself, using the device's real
      factory calibration. Setting it true on hardware would have both this URDF and
      the driver publishing the same edges, producing TF_REPEATED_DATA spam and a
      nondeterministic tree.

      Under Gazebo there is no driver, so nothing else publishes those edges and the
      objection above does not apply - and the simulated camera below has to attach to
      an optical frame, which only exists when the macro emits it. Hence the arg rather
      than a constant. The consequence to remember: sim camera extrinsics are the
      datasheet's NOMINAL values, while the real robot uses its own factory
      calibration, so depth-to-base transforms differ slightly between the two by
      design.

      Mounted on sensor_shelf_link, the deck above the lidar defined in
      circe_sensor_mast.urdf.xacro. That link sits at the deck's TOP surface, so the
      default offsets below are all zero: the camera bolts flat to the shelf,
      centred over the lidar. Must be included AFTER circe_sensor_mast.urdf.xacro.

      Driver-side params live in circe_bringup/config/realsense_d456.yaml. The
      camera_name there ("camera") is what composes camera_link, so it must agree
      with the name= passed to sensor_d455 below.

      *** MEASURE BEFORE DRIVING ***
      camera_mount_* are offsets from the deck surface to the camera's tripod mount
      (the macro's origin is the bottom screw mount, not the lens). A wrong pitch
      here tilts the entire depth cloud and will make flat floor read as an obstacle.

      *** NOTE ON THE y OFFSET ***
      With camera_mount_y = 0 the camera BODY is centred, but camera_link is not:
      the d455 macro puts camera_link at the left imager, 47.5 mm off the case
      centreline (half the 95 mm stereo baseline). So the depth origin sits at
      y = +0.0475. That is physically correct. If you would rather have the depth
      origin on the robot centreline than the camera body, set
      camera_mount_y = -0.0475 instead.
    -->
    <xacro:include filename="$(find realsense2_description)/urdf/_d455.urdf.xacro" />

    <xacro:property name="camera_mount_x" value="0.0" />
    <xacro:property name="camera_mount_y" value="0.0" />
    <xacro:property name="camera_mount_z" value="0.0" />
    <xacro:property name="camera_mount_roll" value="0.0" />
    <!-- Slight downward pitch helps see near obstacles; positive pitch tilts down. -->
    <xacro:property name="camera_mount_pitch" value="0.0" />
    <xacro:property name="camera_mount_yaw" value="0.0" />

    <xacro:arg name="sim_mode" default="false" />

    <xacro:sensor_d455 parent="sensor_shelf_link" name="camera"
                       use_nominal_extrinsics="$(arg sim_mode)">
        <origin xyz="${camera_mount_x} ${camera_mount_y} ${camera_mount_z}"
                rpy="${camera_mount_roll} ${camera_mount_pitch} ${camera_mount_yaw}"/>
    </xacro:sensor_d455>

    <!--
      Simulated D456. Sim only.

      One rgbd_camera rather than separate camera + depth_camera sensors: it emits
      colour, depth and the registered cloud from a single pose, which is what the real
      D456 does and what keeps them mutually consistent.

      *** TOPIC NAMING ***
      gz derives four topics from the single <topic> root below:
          camera/camera/image         camera/camera/depth_image
          camera/camera/points        camera/camera/camera_info
      Those are NOT the driver's names. circe_gazebo/config/gz_bridge.yaml renames each
      onto the hardware-identical ROS name (/camera/camera/color/image_raw and friends)
      by giving different ros_topic_name and gz_topic_name. Change one side and you
      must change the other.

      *** FRAME CONVENTION: VERIFY THIS VISUALLY ***
      gz renders along the sensor's +X, while ROS optical frames are +Z-forward. The
      sensor is attached to camera_link and tagged with the optical frame, which is the
      usual arrangement, but if the cloud shows up rotated ~90 degrees in RViz that is
      the cause. Check it before trusting depth geometry.

      *** RESOLUTION AND RATE ARE DELIBERATELY LOW ***
      The real D456 does 1280x720 at 30 Hz. This runs 424x240 at 10 Hz, and that is a
      deliberate throttle, not an oversight.

      Gazebo renders camera and gpu_lidar sensors on the GPU via EGL. On this machine
      EGL fails in a headless session ("libEGL warning: failed to create dri2 screen")
      and gz silently falls back to a SOFTWARE rasteriser. At 848x480x30 that ate
      enough CPU to starve the rest of the graph: Nav2 lifecycle service calls timed
      out, the EKF logged "Failed to meet update rate", and slam_toolbox hung unable to
      read its own parameters over a service. Nothing looked like a rendering problem;
      it all looked like broken configuration.

      Nothing consumes depth in the costmaps (lidar-only by design), so this costs
      nothing real. Raise it if you fix EGL or only need the camera without Nav2.
    -->
    <xacro:if value="$(arg sim_mode)">
    <gazebo reference="camera_link">
        <sensor name="rgbd_camera" type="rgbd_camera">
            <topic>camera/camera</topic>
            <gz_frame_id>camera_depth_optical_frame</gz_frame_id>
            <update_rate>10</update_rate>
            <always_on>1</always_on>
            <visualize>false</visualize>
            <camera>
                <horizontal_fov>1.518</horizontal_fov>
                <image>
                    <width>424</width>
                    <height>240</height>
                    <format>R8G8B8</format>
                </image>
                <clip>
                    <near>0.2</near>
                    <far>20.0</far>
                </clip>
                <depth_camera>
                    <clip>
                        <near>0.4</near>
                        <far>20.0</far>
                    </clip>
                </depth_camera>
                <noise>
                    <type>gaussian</type>
                    <mean>0.0</mean>
                    <stddev>0.007</stddev>
                </noise>
            </camera>
        </sensor>
    </gazebo>
    </xacro:if>

</robot>
